let C_w C_h be the terminal font cell width and height in pixels.
let R_cell = ratio cell = C_h/C_w. For almost all monospace fonts, R_cell is approx. 2.0; is this what results in the rectangle?
Also let N_cols and N_rows be the number of terminal character cells to render on the screen. This is what you're currently resizing for, and this is exactly what we're trying to calculate here (ideal value).

When rendered on the screen, the physical dimensions are:
width of screen/canvas = N_cols * C_w
height of canvas = N_rows * C_h

If I want the displayed aspect ratio equal to the original image's aspect ratio I can do 
width of screen    W_img      N_cols * C_w      W_img     N_cols     2 W_img
--------------- =  ------ => -------------- =  -----  => --------- = -------
height of screen   H_img      N_rows * C_h      H_img     N_rows      H_img

